typescript:S9383.description:
[root_cause]
A Promise represents the eventual result of an asynchronous operation. When a Promise is created but neither awaited, returned, nor given a
rejection handler, it is said to be "floating": nothing in the code observes whether it eventually resolves or rejects.

This is a frequent source of bugs:

  If the Promise rejects, nothing in the code reacts to the failure. The rejection escapes to the runtime’s global handler instead of the error
  handling around the call site, so recovery, retries, and user-facing messages never run.

  Operations that were meant to happen in a specific order may instead run concurrently or out of order, because the code moved on before the
  asynchronous operation completed. Code that depends on the result may then execute too early, using stale or default data.

  An array of Promises, such as the result of a map() call, is just as easy to leave unhandled as a single Promise, and is a common
  way to accidentally launch several operations without ever finding out if any of them failed.

This rule applies to Promise-valued statements. Promises used where another type is expected, such as in a conditional or a spread, are covered by
S6544.

What is the potential impact?

An unhandled rejection is not contained to the operation that failed. Since Node.js 15, the default --unhandled-rejections mode is throw, which
raises the rejection as an uncaught exception and terminates the process unless an unhandledRejection hook is registered. A single
unobserved background task can therefore take down a server that was otherwise healthy.

[how_to_fix]
Make sure every Promise-returning statement is handled in one of the following ways:

  await it.

  return it, so that the caller becomes responsible for handling it.

  Attach a rejection handler with .catch(), or pass a rejection handler as the second argument to .then().

  If you are dealing with several Promises at once, combine them with Promise.all(), Promise.allSettled(),
  Promise.race(), or Promise.any() before handling the result, instead of leaving an array of Promises unhandled.

If a Promise’s outcome genuinely does not matter and you want to make that explicit, use the void operator on the expression. This
documents that the Promise is intentionally left unobserved. Note that this does not make rejections disappear: it only silences this rule, so it
should be reserved for Promises that you know cannot practically reject, or that are otherwise covered by a global unhandled-rejection handler.

Noncompliant code example

function sendMetrics(payload) {
  fetch('/metrics', { method: 'POST', body: payload }); // Noncompliant: nothing handles a failed request
}

Compliant solution

async function sendMetrics(payload) {
  await fetch('/metrics', { method: 'POST', body: payload });
}

Noncompliant code example

function logAndForget() {
  fetchLogs().then(logs => storeLogs(logs)); // Noncompliant: no rejection handler
}

Compliant solution

function logAndForget() {
  fetchLogs()
    .then(logs => storeLogs(logs))
    .catch(error => reportError(error));
}

Noncompliant code example

function notifyAll(subscribers) {
  subscribers.map(subscriber => subscriber.notify()); // Noncompliant: an array of unhandled Promises
}

Compliant solution

async function notifyAll(subscribers) {
  await Promise.all(subscribers.map(subscriber => subscriber.notify()));
}

Noncompliant code example

function onClick() {
  trackEvent('button_clicked'); // Noncompliant: the Promise is left unhandled and is not marked as intentionally ignored
}

Compliant solution

function onClick() {
  // The analytics call is not expected to fail in a way that matters here.
  void trackEvent('button_clicked');
}

